Suggestion on AD side does not help:

On the *Domain Controllers* (GPO applied to DC OU):

# Open Group Policy Editor and go to:
*Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → System → KDC → Configure hash algorithms for certificate logon*

* Also set the same in:
*Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → System → Kerberos → Configure hash algorithms for certificate logon*
* In these policies, explicitly set the allowed hashes to include what your Heimdal client uses (often you’ll need to include *SHA-1* just to confirm diagnosis, then tighten later).

See also [https://lists.samba.org/archive/cifs-protocol/2025-August/004550.html|https://lists.samba.org/archive/cifs-protocol/2025-August/004550.html|smart-link] 

MIT patching: [https://github.com/krb5/krb5/pull/1411|https://github.com/krb5/krb5/pull/1411|smart-link] 

Possible workaround (ECC crypto): [https://issues.redhat.com/browse/RHEL-74295|https://issues.redhat.com/browse/RHEL-74295]

[https://windowsforum.com/threads/microsoft-flips-kerberos-default-from-rc4-to-aes-sha1-in-active-directory-by-2026.393966/|https://windowsforum.com/threads/microsoft-flips-kerberos-default-from-rc4-to-aes-sha1-in-active-directory-by-2026.393966/|smart-link] 

The problem can be reproduced with our new test domain HINT.LOCAL

While working on [https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-13842|https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-13842|smart-link]  I have noticed that between 12.7.5 and 12.8.rc.5 the edid has changed for the internal display of the LENOVO T14 GEN6. 
Since we rely on the edid data to identify the display we treat the internal display in 12.8 as a different display from the one we configured in 12.7 and thus the configuration is lost. 

Details: 
Running {{$ get_drm edid edp1 manufacturer}} on 12.7 returns {{Chimei Innolux Corporation}}
On the other side on 12.8 it returns {{Lenovo Group Limited}}
Also running {{$ edid-decode /sys/class/drm/card0/card0-eDP-1/edid}}
gives different results between 12.7 and 12.8
[^12.7 edid] [^12.8.rc5 edid]

