While working on some touchpad issues [https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-12008|https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-12008|smart-link] [https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-13339|https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-13339|smart-link] I found some logic inconsistencies in the LibinputHandler. In practice, I cannot see any issues, but after talking with [~accountid:712020:3f68fc46-4312-421c-883d-b96b5bc1e2bb], this is clear these are typos. 

{noformat}devices_changed = is_keyboard(device) || is_mouse(device) || is_mouse(device) || devices_changed;
...
} else if (is_mouse(device)) {
  ++touchpads;
}
... 
devices_changed = is_keyboard(device) || is_mouse(device) || is_mouse(device) || devices_changed;{noformat}

In Serializer.cpp: {{Serializer::getJsonObject}} the tokenizer is allocated but never freed. A simple json_tokener_free after the usage of the tokenizer should do the job. 



{noformat}auto tok = json_tokener_new();
...
json_tokener_free(tok);{noformat}



P.S.: As a side note due to the ongoing serialzer discussion about trailing 0 bytes, libigelserialize uses internally the json-C interface, which means it will already most probably stop at the first \0 it encounters, which means a valid formatted std::string with a \0 somewhere in the middle will fail to be parsed because the tokener will stop.

