column58:
The x11 keymaps in IGEL OS map the Alt+Print key to the “Sys_Req” keysym. This causes problems in remote Windows sessions where “Alt+Print” is a common keyboard shortcut.

{{xmodmap -pke | grep Print}}

{{keycode 107 = Print Sys_Req Print Sys_Req}}

We should change that to this for all keyboard layouts to this:

{{xmodmap -e "keycode 107 = Print Print Print Print"}}



Note that this is also related to [https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-11893|https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX8-11893|smart-link] since with sysrq enabled in the kernel the Alt+Print key combination is not propagated to X11 at all! Therefore make sure that sysrq is disabled, ie. by “cat /proc/sys/kernel/sysrq" to print “0”. If not, execute “echo 0 > /proc/sys/kernel/sysrq" to disable sysrq in the kernel.

There is a memory leak for [pcsc-lite|https://itga-bitbucket.igel.local/projects/LXTHP/repos/pcsc-lite/browse] in OS11.10.211. The leak is gone in version OS11.10.233 due to an update in {{pcsc-lite}}. However, this version is broken for Honeywell (see [https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX7-10835|https://igel-jira.atlassian.net/browse/LX7-10835|smart-link] ). 

The working version with memory leak:

/system7_images/standard/honeywell/11.10.211/src/igel_gpl:*380c3f81dde5f989f275fa6bed31b6761418a396* ssh://git@itga-bitbucket.igel.local:7999/lxthp/pcsc-lite.git master

The not working version without memory leak:

/system7_images/standard/honeywell/11.10.233/src/igel_gpl:*798bf8b2f23fd9620a3ac014668e052549c750a0* ssh://git@itga-bitbucket.igel.local:7999/lxthp/pcsc-lite.git master

The memory leak is caused by the patch {{008-IGEL-always-return-SCardGetStatusChange.diff}} which introduces the following if-statement:

{noformat}if ((unsigned)newReaderCount != ((currReader->dwCurrentState) >> 16))
{
	int readerEvents;

	Log1(PCSC_LOG_INFO, "Reader count changed");
	currentReaderCount = newReaderCount << 16;

	if (SCARD_S_SUCCESS == getReaderEvents(currentContextMap, &readerEvents))
	{
		// store readerEvents in .dwEventState high word
		currReader->dwEventState = (currReader->dwEventState & 0xFFFF) + (readerEvents << 16);
	}

	currReader->dwEventState |= SCARD_STATE_CHANGED;
	dwBreakFlag = 1;
}{noformat}

The leak likely comes from the fact that this function is called again and again and enters this if-statement. So the leaked memory is likely happening on the outside in another part of the code. Removing this block makes the memory leak small enough to not be detected using the debug tool. 

Update:

This if-statement is essential for smartcard reader detection - removing it also led to the reader not being detected on startup for honeywell.

Found another solution to fix smartcard reader detection, see patch [https://itga-bitbucket.igel.local/projects/LXTHP/repos/pcsc-lite/commits/6643ca6ec600407c4122ad8e9f83feb444ec2e06|https://itga-bitbucket.igel.local/projects/LXTHP/repos/pcsc-lite/commits/6643ca6ec600407c4122ad8e9f83feb444ec2e06]

h3. Environment

* Honeywell OS 11.10.211
* Smart Card reader
* RDP session

h3. Steps to reproduce

# Plug in smart card reader
# Open RDP session
# See leaked memory for {{pcscd}} using {{/etc/igel/igel_debug_tools/amdgpu-debug.sh}}

