column58:
After removing Tiger VNC viewer from base system, the following libraries are also removed from base system:

{{libfltk1.3}}
{{libfltk-images1.3}}


The app should contain these libs.

The singleton files are not deleted and therefore chromium does not start. 

{noformat}514: utils.py -> get_chromium_and_pwa_profile_path() -> profile_path = [dir for dir in config_path.iterdir() if dir.is_dir()][0]{noformat}

The function always returns the first folder and therefore never the Chromium folder.

MSAL related projects, like Intune and AVD, expect the login collection (with alias)

{noformat}/org/freedesktop/secrets/aliases/login{noformat}

by default to be present. Provide this by default like we do already with our 'default' alias/collection.

AVD 1.3.0 with MSAL would use the Microsoft Identity Broker that comes with the Intune app in order to cache and re-use user credentials and in order to avoid asking users multiple times for their credentials for just connecting to one single AVD session! Therefore the AVD app with MSAL heavily depends on the Microsoft Identity Broker.

But unfortunately this current fails on IGEL OS. Could be related to the IGEL secret bridge again. But that is just a wild guess. Although the crucial error message from the journal when MSAL tries to use the identity broker says this:

{{Nov 04 18:03:23 ITCC217F548DBBB microsoft-identity-broker[12084]: W/StorageEncryptionManager#:decrypt: [2024-11-04 17:03:23 - thread_id: 27, correlation_id: }}
{{1f582807-b970-4e08-b83f-945c53b256b4 - ] Failed to decrypt with key:LinuxBrokerRegularUserSecretKey thumbprint : 7v-kNtIH4sVSJXKNgV00AMqrrzaraAZKc9fss88IvKE}}


Please have a look into this. This is quite crucial for future AVD/W365 releases when MSAL is supposed to be used!

See attached journal.txt for more context/details.

The intune-agent behaves odd when using custom compliance scripts

{noformat}SettingStatus {
	rule_id: "2acad966-8edc-43f4-a47f-b03d7c34c0a2",
	setting_definition_item_id: "linux_customcompliance_discoveryscript",
	expected_value: "Unknown",
	actual_value: "{\"ums_fingerprint_sha256\":\"90:40:EF:F8:64:DA:5E:55:22:FE:FF:94:CC:39:66:44:55:3E:E3:9C:36:10:11:4A:5B:16:88:AF:6D:81:BF:21\"}\n",
	new_compliance_state: Unknown,
	old_compliance_state: Unknown
}{noformat}

"Built-in" compliance rules report their status as expected:

{noformat}SettingStatus {
	rule_id: "270eb9cb-a735-4744-a234-e99ce54e3e80",
	setting_definition_item_id: "linux_distribution_alloweddistros_item_$type",
	expected_value: "ubuntu",
	actual_value: "ubuntu",
	new_compliance_state: Compliant,
	old_compliance_state: Unknown
}{noformat}

The custom compliance {{expected_value}} is always {{Unknown}} eventhough it is defines as string, in this case the fingerprint {{90:40:ef:..}}

-We need to use a different indicator from intune-agent stdout to determine compliance.-

EDIT: Turned out this IWS compliance information is only in the stdout from intune-portal, not from intune-agent! We only have the intune-agent run in the background, intune-portal is (or will be) running in the foreground usually only during onboarding. We need to find a different way to get the full compliance state when custom scripts are involved…

I've seen this status when compliant with built-in and custom rules (EDIT: only available in intune-portal stdout, not intune-agent):

{noformat}2024-11-04 17:09:00 INFO Compliant status indicated by IWS (no compliance issues requiring remedation){noformat}

Maybe use this in addition to the {{new_compliance_state}} checks.

column363:
12/Nov/24 3:06 PM;712020:590271f0-d8b1-4789-b812-dca0b6ac1880;Yes, I'd like to avoid patching cpython. Would be using an apropriate timeout to subprocess.run() be an option for you?

{noformat}import subprocess
try:
    result = subprocess.run(["command"], capture_output=True, timeout=10)
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
    print("Child process reached timeout"){noformat}

Alternatively one could use subprocess.Popen() directly, which gives more control on the child process and the option to avoid the blocking subprocess.communicate().

