column368:
05/Sep/24 2:54 PM;712020:4db56460-3e39-4b8e-8a37-90ff082b0c08;[~accountid:712020:6a3446d6-af32-4ed2-988e-cc80a93d769d] [~accountid:712020:df0542bb-113f-45fd-bf10-d8b3a67211d5] : Ok, I think I got the problem. The issue here is that the GTK file dialog of the screenshot tool and in general of all GTK applications does automount USB devices! When USB access control is not enabled and the default rule is not set to “deny”, it is allowed to do so. Which is fine from the USB access control perspective. So no change required for the USB access control here.

The point here is more that the device is mounted by the generic GTK file dialog, although automount in the IGEL setup is disabled. But since this is builtin GTK file dialog functionality, this would be needed to be takled from GTK. These command line settings would achive that:

{{gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.media-handling automount false}}
{{gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.media-handling automount-open false}}

I don’t think this is a blocker though and this behaviour is there since GTK has that feature included and eventually other UI toolkits do a similiar thing.

[~accountid:712020:6a3446d6-af32-4ed2-988e-cc80a93d769d] Shall we set the automount and automount-open options for GTK to avoid that behaviour in future releases? I don’t see this as a security issue since only USB access control can safely prevent applications from accessing USB devices!!!

